<h2>The incident and the trial</h2>
<p>On 4 August 2004, a scooter carrying Rs 3 lakh in cash was intercepted near the Azadpur Mandi. The pillion rider, carrying the money, was shot and injured in the robbery. The investigation swept in four accused; the prosecution’s theory against Jiya Lal — arrested about two weeks later, on 17 August 2004 — was that he kept watch during the robbery and that the looted money was shared at his jhuggi, a detail sourced entirely from the disclosure statements of co-accused. The trial court convicted him with the others under Sections 394, 395, 397, 307, 120-B IPC and, for harbouring, Section 216-A, sentencing him to concurrent terms including five years’ rigorous imprisonment for robbery.</p>

<h2>Where the evidence came apart</h2>
<p>The appeal, filed in 2012, was finally heard in October 2026. Justice Ravinder Dudeja examined each strand of the prosecution case and found none that held.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>The complainant</strong><p>PW-1, the scooter rider, could not identify any assailant at all.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The injured witness</strong><p>PW-4 identified the appellant for the first time in court, years later, with no test identification parade — and no explanation for why none was held. His account also shifted: in examination-in-chief he attributed the blocking of the scooter to a co-accused; in cross-examination, to the appellant.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The disclosures</strong><p>The co-accused statements about sharing the loot at the appellant’s jhuggi led to no recovery and no discovered fact, making them inadmissible against him.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The residue</strong><p>No recovery from the appellant, no evidence of common intention or conspiracy, and nothing to sustain the harbouring charge.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court’s conclusion was unvarnished: the conviction was founded only on suspicion, and the prosecution had failed to establish guilt beyond reasonable doubt. The appeal was allowed, the conviction and sentence set aside, and the appellant acquitted of all charges, his bail bond remaining valid for six weeks.</p>

<h2>The law on first-time dock identification</h2>
<p>A test identification parade is not a legal prerequisite to conviction — courts have said so repeatedly. But where the witness had no prior acquaintance with the accused, the incident was brief and violent, and the identification surfaces for the first time in the dock long after the event, the absence of a TIP strips the identification of the assurance it needs. The prosecution can survive a missing parade only when something else supplies that assurance: prior familiarity, distinctive features promptly described, or corroborating recovery. This record had none of those anchors.</p>

<h2>What the case illustrates for appellate practice</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>Robbery and dacoity convictions often rest on a lattice of identification and disclosure evidence that looks imposing in aggregate but dissolves strand by strand. An effective appeal isolates each element — who identified, when, with what prior opportunity; what each disclosure actually discovered; what was recovered from whom — and tests it against the settled rules of admissibility. As this judgment shows, the appellate court will not let the strands borrow strength from one another when none can stand alone.</p>
</div>
<p>The decision also reflects a sobering institutional reality: the appeal took fourteen years to decide, and the incident dates back twenty-two. For an accused, the discipline of keeping bail bonds, addresses and representation current across such a span is itself part of the defence.</p>
